Flying MOFs: Polyamine-containing fluidized MOF/ $\mathsf{s i o}_{2}$ hybrid materials for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture from post-combustion flue gas
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x www.rsc.org/
Solid-state synthesis ensures a high loading and well-dispersed growth of a large collection of metal–organic framework (MOF) nanostructures within a series of commercially available mesoporous silica. This approach provides a general, highly efficient, scalable, environmentally friendly, and inexpensive strategy to render MOFs into a fluidized form, thereby allowing their application in fluidized-bed reactors for diverse applications, such as $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture from post-combustion flue gas. A collection of polyamine-impregnated $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ hybrid sorbents were evaluated for ${\mathsf C}{\mathsf O}_{2}$ capture under simulated flue gas conditions in a packed-bed reactor. Hybrid sorbents containing a moderate loading of $(Z_{\mathsf{n}})Z|\mathsf{F}{-}8$ are the most promising sorbents in terms of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capacity and long-term stability (up to 250 cycles under the presence of contaminants: $S{\mathsf{O}}_{2},$ $\mathsf{N O}_{\mathsf{x}}$ and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}$ ) and were succesfully prepared at the kilogram scale. These hybrid sorbents demonstrated excelent fluidizability and performance under the relevant process conditions in a visible fluidized-bed reactor. Moreover, a biochemically inspired strategy for covalently linking polyamines to $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ through strong phosphine bonds has been firstly introduced first in this work as a powerful and highly versatile post-synthesis modification for MOF chemistry, thus providing a novel alternative towards more stable ${\mathsf C}{\mathsf O}_{2}$ solid sorbents.
## Introduction
${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture from both coal- and natural gas-fired power plants using solid sorbents has been proposed as the most promising alternative to the current amine-solution scrubbing technologies because of their lower energy requirements for sorbent regeneration, as well as less amine evaporation and equipment corrosion.1 For this specific application, the state-ofthe-art for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ solid sorbents is mainly based on polyamines containing more than two ethylamine monomers (i.e. diethylenediamine, tetraethylenepentamine or polyethyleneimine) either 1) confined or polymerized in situ from arizidine within the mesoporous silica cavities2 or 2) coordinated to the open metal sites of metal–organic frameworks $({\mathsf{M O F s}})^{3}$ , which are preferred among supports, including zeolites, polymers or activated carbons.4
In these solid composites, polyamines selectively adsorb ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ via chemisorption at the primary and secondary amines. MOFs also have the capability to absorb ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ via physisorption, either at the Lewis-acid open metal sites5 or via molecular size confinement even under the presence of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}},{\mathsf{\Omega}}^{6}$ which have both demonstrated excellent capacity and selectivity over N. However, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ physisorption is reduced or limited by temperature, which leaves polyamine-impregnated MOFs as the most suitable strategy involving MOFs to prepare solid sorbents for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture from flue gas conditions typically operating at temperatures $40~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and greater, although some promising results have been recently reported.7
In the race for developing more energy-efficient and competitive processes involving amine-containing solid sorbents for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture from post-combustion flue gas streams, fluidized-bed reactors (FBRs) are proposed as a more energy-efficient ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture technology than packed-bed configurations due to their improved heat and mass transfer.8 In this way, several polyamine-containing solid sorbents using mesoporous silica as fluidized support have been successfully tested in an FBR at pilot plant scale.9 Nevertheless, the current fluidized solid sorbents show capacities below $10\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture and suffer long-term deactivation via leaching of the active adsorbent phase because of the weak interactions via hydrogen bonds between polyamine and the support.
To the best of our knowledge, MOF–based ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ solid sorbents have never been studied in a fluidized-bed configuration because of the absence or limitation of some essential features required for operating under these conditions, such as fluidizability and attrition resistance. Therefore, it is quite important to develop a general strategy for shaping MOFs into a fluidized form, which may confer MOFs with those required physicochemical properties. Furthermore, additional synergistic features can be originated from MOFs and support intimal combination, such as hierarchical mesoporosity or microporosity, which may host and disperse more active polyamines while providing them with more long-term stability via either coordination bond at open metal sites or covalent bond at free functional groups.10
Our group has recently developed an elegant approach to selectively confine MOF nanocrystals within mesoporous materials via novel solid-state synthesis, which allows the preparation of hybrid materials containing a high loading (up to $40\mathrm{\:wt\%})$ ) of the smallest nanocrystals yet reported for any MOF structure within a wide collection of mesoporous supports, also named HyperMOFs.11 The resulting hybrid materials show improved features from the combination of a silica scaffold protecting and supporting the MOF nanocrystallites within its porous network. Some of these improved features include improved attrition resistance, excellent fluidizability and handling, as well as the enhanced catalytic activity recently reported by our group.12 This insight encouraged us to prepare a novel kind of “flying” MOFs by using cheap, commercially available mesoporous silica as fluidizable carrier for MOFs.
Herein, a collection of polyamine-impregnated $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ was tested for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture under simulated flue gas conditions in a packed-bed reactor (PBR) and compared with the-state-ofart sorbents composed by polyamines on both “fluidized” silica and “non-fluidized” bulk MOFs. This systematic study reveals the correlations between the features of ${\mathsf{M O F/S i O}}_{2},$ such as loading, composition, and functionality of the confined MOFs, and the performance of impregnated polyamines for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture. To study their viability for larger scale, the most promising materials have been evaluated for long-term stability in a PBR and a visual fluidized-bed reactor (vFBR). In addition, a cost evaluation study was conducted by our group to compare with state-of-the-art MOF–based solid sorbents. Furthermore, a novel strategy for covalently bonding polyamines to the fluidized $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ hybrid materials was introduced during this work to precisely tailor the new generation of HyperMOF hybrid materials for specific applications.
## Experimental
## Fluidized MOF/ $\mathsf{s i o}_{2}$ hybrid materials synthesis
The preparation and characterization steps for all of the ${\mathsf{M O F}}/{\mathsf{S i O}}_{2}$ hybrid materials included in this work were conducted by following our recently reported procedure for solid-state synthesis of MOFs within mesoporous materials.11 Moderate concentrations of MOFs on $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ were also achieved by incipient wetness impregnation of the MOF precursor in dimethylforamide (DMF) or MeOH, as described in the Supporting Information.
## Bulk MOFs synthesis
Bulk MOFs included in this work were prepared by following the reported procedures, such as $(\mathsf{C r})\mathsf{M l L}\mathsf{-}101(\mathsf{S O}_{3}\mathsf{H}),\mathsf{13}$ ZIF-8,14 (Zr)UiO-66 $(N H_{2})$ ,15 $\ensuremath{\mathsf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}_{2}$ (dobpdc),16 and NbOFFIVE-1-Ni.17
