Polyamine covalently bonded to $(\mathsf{Z r})\mathsf{U i O-}66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ via postsynthesis modification with tris(hydroxymethyl)phosphine
In a first step, $_{2\tt8}$ of a previously evacuated sample of $4.2~\mathsf{w t\%}$ of $\mathrm{(}\mathrm{Zr)UiO{-}}66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ was exposed overnight to the vapors of $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of tris(hydroxymethyl)phosphine (THP) at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ inside a Schlenk flask under vacuum to enhance the degree of amine functionalization (because THP can be easily oxidized when $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ is present). The material was thoroughly washed with dry ${\mathsf{C H C l}}_{3},$ and then evacuated at ${}^{120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}}$ under vacuum. Finally, $35\mathrm{\ut\%}$ of polyethylenimine (PEI) was introduced to the modified material as previously described for preparing polyamine-confined sorbents.
## $\mathbf{co}_{2}$ capacity and stability testing of solid sorbents in a PBR
During the next portion of the study, $_{2\tt g}$ of sorbent were loaded into a PBR and tested for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture under simulated flue gas conditions. Specifically, the conditions were as follows: $C O_{2}=15$ $v/\ v\%$ , $\mathsf{O}_{2}=4.5~\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}\%$ , ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}=5.6{\sf v}/{\sf v\%}$ in balance with ${\sf N}_{2}$ at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ during 45 min for the adsorption step, and ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}=5.6{\sf v}/{\sf v\%}$ in balance with ${\sf N}_{2}$ at ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ during 45 min for the regeneration step. To evaluate the stability of the sorbents under the presence of contaminants, controlled concentration of $S O_{2}$ (50 or 200 ppm), $\mathsf{N O}_{\mathsf{x}}$ (20 ppm of NO and 180 ppm of ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ ), or ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ $(1\upnu/\upnu\%)$ were added during the adsorption step.
## Fluidization and stability test of solid sorbents in an FBR
During the next portion of the study, $_{50\textbf{g}}$ of sorbent were loaded into a vFBR. The adsorption was performed by exposing the sorbent with 2 SLPM of $15\ \mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\%$ of $\mathsf{C O}_{2},\mathsf{8}\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}\%$ of $H_{2}O$ in balance with ${\sf N}_{2}$ at $40^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ until the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ breakthrough was observed and the outlet ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentration was stable. Then, between the adsorption and regeneration steps, the reactor was purged with ${\sf N}_{2}$ to remove the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ in the gas-phase remaining in the system. Finally, the regeneration step was performed by treating the sorbent with $10\mathrm{~v~}/\mathrm{v}\%$ of $H_{2}O$ in ${\sf N}_{2}$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . This treatment was maintained until the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was completely desorbed from the sorbent. To evaluate the fluidizability, the sorbents were exposed to $80\ \mathrm{v/v\%}$ of $H_{2}O$ in balance with ${\sf N}_{2}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The pressure drops across the FBR of each sorbent were also examined by varying the ${\sf N}_{2}$ flow from 0.5 to 4.8 SLPM.
Complete descriptions of the PBR and vFBR, as well as the experiment procedures are included in the Supporting Information and in our previous work.18
## Results and discussion
$\mathbf{co}_{2}$ adsorption performance of polyamine-containing MOF $1\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ under simulated flue gas conditions: Initial study for (Cr)MIL$\mathbf{101}(\mathsf{S O_{3}H})/\mathsf{S i O_{2}}$
$(\mathsf{C r})\mathsf{M l L-}101(50_{3}\mathsf{H})$ has been selectively grown in a commercially fluidized mesoporous silica (Silica[A]) at various loadings, such as 5, 19 and $40\ \mathsf{w t\%}$ , by following our recently reported procedure.11 This MOF was selected as an initial candidate because of its well-known chemical and thermal stability and because of the presence of functional groups and available open metal sites for coordinating polyamines, which has been recently reported as an approach for preparing polyaminecontaining MOFs for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture.19 As shown in Fig. 1, the resulting fluidized $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ hybrids exhibited similar physicochemical properties than their bulk counterparts because ${\sf N}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption was proportional to the MOF loading (measured by X-ray fluorescence [XRF]), thus confirming that those are preserved after integrating MOF nanocrystals within the mesoporous cavities. The presence, loading, and homogeneity of MOF nanocrystals confined on these MOF/ $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ hybrid materials were further confirmed by XRF, X-ray diffraction (XRD), Fourier transform infrared (FTIR), confocal microscopy, transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and scanning electron microscopy (SEM), as described in Fig. 1 and in the Supporting Information.
Next, $_{2\textbf{g}}$ of those composites were tested in a PBR under simulated flue gas conditions for 10 cycles to evaluate ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity and stability. As shown in Fig. 2, TEPA coordinated on both 19 and $40\ \mathsf{w t\%}$ MOF/ $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{S i O}_{2}}$ exhibited better performance and amine use for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture compared with pure bulk MOF. $43\%$ of amine utilization was calculated for $19\ \mathsf{w t}\%$ , $26\%$ , and for $40~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and only $14\%$ for bulk MOF under these experimental conditions, that is, considering that four of the TEPA molecules may coordinate ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ while one is coordinated to the MOF (Table S2 in the Supplemental Information). Lower ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity was measured for materials fully functionalized by shorter polyamines because of their lower number of amines per molecule, such as DETA or ethylenediamine (Table S2 in the Supplemental Information). This evidence shows the beneficial effects of having a moderate concentration of well-dispersed MOF nanocrystals anchoring polyamines to enhance the accessibility and efficiency of the amine adsorption sites under simulated flue gas conditions.
However, the second approach via confinement within the pores leads to higher loading of active polyamine, and thereby higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacities because the hierarchical mesoporosity and microporosity of the $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ hybrid materials can be impregnated with up to $35\ \mathsf{w t\%}$ of branched polyamine (PEI), $\mathsf{M}_{\mathsf{w}}=$ approximately 800) without revealing signs of stickiness, which would avoid their application in fluidized beds.18 The infiltration of PEI on MOF/ $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ microporosity and mesoporosity has been confirmed by ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms, as compared in
Fig. 2b for 19 wt% of $(C\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}})\mathsf{M}|\mathsf{L}-101(\mathsf{S}\mathsf{O}_{3}\mathsf{H})/\mathsf{S}\mathsf{i}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ containing anchored TEPA (occupying microporosity) and confined $35\mathrm{wt\%}$ of PEI (occupying both microporosity and mesoporosity). Thus, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity for confined PEI is also highly dependent on the MOF loading because the presence of 19 and $40\mathrm{\ut\%}$ of MOF within the mesoporous silica reduces the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the amines compared with bare silica. This inhibition phenomena may be attributed to the interaction of PEI amines with both open metal sites and free functional groups at the confined MOF and the defective sites located at the MOF boundary inherent to the nanocrystalline domains. Therefore, ${\mathsf C}{\mathsf O}_{2}$ capacity proportionally drops with the MOF loading because the lower capacity was found for $40\upmu\upnu\upnu\%$ MOF loading. Surprisingly, PEI infiltrated on a HyperMOF containing only 5 $w t\%$ of MOF leads to a slight enhancement $(c a.10\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity (Fig. 2C). However, additional experiments confining $35\ \mathsf{w t\%}$ of TEPA on $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ materials revealed a high deactivation because of the leaching of non-coordinated TEPA molecules during regeneration step at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ in the presence of wet nitrogen. This finding was also observed for 35 $w t\%$ of TEPA impregnated on the same bulk MOF and as described later in this manuscript for other impregnated short polyamines (see Fig. 3 and Table 1). Therefore, the use of TEPA as a polyamine was discarded for this application. Branched PEI was found to be more stable for fluidized MOF hybrid sorbents under simulated flue gas conditions.
## Scope for $35w t\%$ of PEI confined HyperMOFs
We considered to extend this study to other MOF nanocrystals grown within mesoporous silica to evaluate the effects that MOF composition and loading have on the activity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture of a defined amount of impregnated PEI. Furthermore, the following scope of hybrid solid sorbents containing various loadings of a collection of chemically stable MOFs was selected to provide a better understanding about the boost in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity observed for moderate MOF loadings. In doing so, MOFs exhibiting different metals at the nodes (with both coordinatively unsaturated and saturated sites) and ligands (with and without free functionality) were prepared at various MOF loadings for this scope. To compare our fluidized hybrid materials with the most promising polyamine-containing MOF–based ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ sorbents, bulk $\mathsf{M g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) decorated with up to 32 wt% of $\mathsf{N},\mathsf{N}^{\prime}$ -dimethylethylenediamine (mmen) was prepared according to the literature16 and tested under dry (no ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O})$ and simulated flue gas conditions. A similar characterization was conducted for all resulting hybrid solid sorbents listed in Table 1 (also, see the Supporting Information).
As shown in Table 1, similar tendency was observed for 35 $w t\%$ of PEI impregnated on HyperMOFs hybrid materials at various loadings compared with the results presented in Fig. 2c. In all of the cases, a boost in the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity was found for ca. $5w t\%$ of MOF loading compared with just a PEI impregnated on pure silica, whereas lower ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity and higher deactivation were measured for higher MOF loadings. Therefore, initial ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ sorption capacity of PEI amines is improved by the presence of MOF nanocrystals, but at moderate loadings, because a concomitant inhibition mechanism is significantly affecting the adsorption performance for excessive presence of MOF. The irreversible inhibition pathways may be one or a combination of one of three scenarios. The first scenario involves neutralization of the PEI amines by defective ligands and/or coordinatively unsaturated metal sites. The second scenario involves oxidation of the PEI amines by MOF metal components at the nodes at high temperatures. The last scenario involves hindrance or aggregation of the PEI amines because of confineViemweAnrticlewiOtnhliine microporous cavities. DOI: 10.1039/C7SC05372J
By comparing the results obtained for (Zn)ZIF-8 (entries 12 through 16) or (Zn)ZIF-7 (entry 17), which consist of imidazolate-type ligands, with the remainder of the carboxylate-type MOFs, such as (Cr)MIL-101 $({\mathsf{S O}}_{3}{\mathsf{H}})$ (entries 2 through 5) or $(\mathsf{Z r})\mathsf{U i O}\mathsf{-}66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ (entries 7 through 11), the initial ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity and the deactivation rate are less affected by the MOF loading. Thus, boundary crystal defects in the MOF nanocrystals containing carboxylate-type ligands may neutralize more numbers of PEI amines than MOFs consisting of N-type ligands. Therefore, better long-term stability was observed for (Zn)ZIFs compared with carboxylate-containing MOFs that exhibit higher deactivation in all cases. However, materials containing Co (entry 22) or Fe (entry 25) at the metal nodes show the highest deactivation rates compared with their isostructural counterpart containing Zn (entry 12) or Cr (entry 24) for the same MOF loadings, respectively. This finding can be attributed to a superior activity of those metals for catalytic amine oxidation.
In all cases, $_{2\tt g}$ of solid sorbent were loaded into the PBR blended with $\mathsf{S i C}_{4}$ particles as inert with good thermo-conductivity, and $35\mathrm{wt\%}$ TEPA was used for bulk MOFs instead of PEI. a ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity at the second cycle measured in a PBR under simulated flue gas conditions. b 10 cycles ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption deactivation.  c MOF loading calculated by using XRF. d Sorbent was measured under dry conditions. $e32\mathrm{wt\%}$ mmen was used instead of PEI. f No polyamine was used.
## Bulk versus hybrid MOFs
To the best of our knowledge, literature describing the performance and stability of polyamines confined within bulk MOFs for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture under simulated flue gas conditions in a PBR is very limited. In this current work, such study was included for selected bulk MOFs (Table 1, see entries 5, 6, 11, 15, 16, 18, 19, 20, and 21) to compare the performance with the stability observed for our fluidized ${\mathsf{M O F}}/{\mathsf{S i O}}_{2}$ hybrid sorbents under the same conditions (see Fig. 3). A clear inhibition of the amine capacity was observed for mmen- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\sf M}{\bf g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) when simulated flue gas conditions were used. The presence of $5.6~\mathsf{w t\%}$ of $H_{2}O$ in the stream leads to a reduction in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity from an excellent capacity of ca. $14\upmu\upnu\uptau\%$ (entry 19) measured under dry condition in both thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and PBR (confirming reported value of $15.6~\mathsf{w t\%})$ ) to $6.2~\mathsf{w t\%}$ (entry 18) under simulated flue gas conditions. According to elementary analysis, the N contained is also notably reduced from $10.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ (confirming reported value of $10.3\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) to $6.9\mathrm{wt\%}$ ( $31\%$ N loss) after 10 cycles, probably because of a progressive displacement by $H_{2}O$ molecules of volatile diamines, which are evaporated during the regeneration stage at ${120}^{\circ}{\mathsf C},$ as also recently described in literature.7
In the same way, deactivation was observed for bulk $(Z n)Z|F-$ 8 (entries 15 and 16) and $(\mathsf{C r})\mathsf{M l L-}101(\mathsf{S O}_{3}\mathsf{H})$ (entries 5 and 6) in the presence of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}},$ although ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity is quite similar under both “wet” and dry conditions for (Zn)ZIF-8, but is lower for dry $(C r)\mathsf{M L-}101(50_{3}\mathsf{H})$ compared with “wet” conditions. In the same way, very low deactivation (i.e. ${<}1\%$ ) was measured for all polyamine-containing bulk MOFs under dry conditions. Moreover, an FTIR analysis of fresh and used TEPA impregnated in $(C r)\mathsf{M l L-}101(50_{3}\mathsf{H})$ and (Zn)ZIF-8 revealed some differences that may be attributed to the degradation of MOF structures in presence of polyamine promoted by either $\phantom{+}0_{2},$ ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O},$ temperature or combination of them (Fig. S6 in the Supplemental Information). In the same way, similar stability issues under simulated flue gas conditions were reported in literature20 for TEPA-impregnated bulk MOFs and were criticized by researchers21 for bulk MOFs adsorbing ${\mathsf C}{\mathsf O}_{2}$ by physisorption, such as (Mg)MOF-74 and SIFSIX-3-Cu, which show a drastic drop in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity in a few cycles compared with highly stable copper silicalite. Nevertheless, NbOFFIVE-1- Ni overcame these observed stability issues because of its hydrophobicity (entries 20 and 21), although the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity was lower and reversibly dropped by $28\%$ in the presence of 5.6 $v/v\%$ of $H_{2}O$ (Fig. S8 in the Supplemental Information).
Another hypothetical reason for this inherent drop in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity could be caused by the loose polyamine dispersion and subsequent mutual aggregation that leads to an auto-inhibiting effect of the amine sorption sites. This effect was observed for our hybrid materials containing more than $40\upmu\upnu\uptau\%$ of PEI and for
PEI confined on pure mesoporous silicas exhibitingVibeowtAhrt sclemOanlliener and larger pore sizes (e.g. 9.5 nm in SBA-15DOoIr: 1402.1n03m9 /iCn7SiCli0c5a3[7D2]J, respectively, see Fig. S9 in the Supplemental Information) than mesoporous Silica(A). In comparison, Silica(A) exhibited the optimal polyamine dispersion and was also enhanced by the presence of MOF nanocrystals. In addition, this effect may also be attributed to a hygroscopic inhibition that leads to a reduction of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity because of the accumulation of $H_{2}O$ around the highly hydroscopic polyamine-impregnated solids. Therefore, these major limitations for direct application of polyamine-containing bulk MOFs under simulated flue conditions are efficiently overcome by using a moderate concentration of MOF nanocrystals dispersed within mesoporous silica. Doing so will raise the adsorption efficiency of the impregnated amines while avoiding the inhibition caused by polyamine aggregation and hydroscopic hindrance, which further encourage the use of our $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ hybrid solid sorbents for this application.
## Boost in capacity
Several compounds have been used as additives for improving the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity of PEI confined on mesoporous silica, such as surfactants,22 which enhance the PEI dispersion along the mesoporous cavities, thus active PEI amines are better exposed at the gas–solid interface. Otherwise, poor dispersion of polyamines commonly involves that a significant number of amines can be sterically hindered by their mutual aggregation by intermolecular H bonds, which inherently leads to the inhibition of their ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and the limited $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ diffusion through the amine-impregnated pores. In the same way, well-dispersed MOF nanocrystals may also act as a dispersing agent (see Fig. 4), thus providing a more active surface area to stabilize PEI molecules. MOF nanocrystals within the silica reduce the mesoporosity while those cavities are filled with microporous crystallites, which can interact with PEI molecules by bonding or confining them. Nevertheless, we have determined that excessive loadings of MOF lead to proportional inhibition of the PEI amines. Therefore, an optimal compromise between polyamine dispersion and inhibition was found for ca. $5w t\%$ of MOF loading.
To provide insight regarding the influence that the presence of
MOF nanocrystals causes on the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ dynamics of impregnated PEI, the adsorption–desorption profiles for 35 wt% of PEI confined on $4.6\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $(Z\mathsf{n})Z!\mathsf{F}{\cdot}8/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ and bare $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ at the tenth cycle are compared in Fig. 5. The adsorption breakthrough profile for the MOF–containing solid sorbent shows a superior ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption compared with PEI/ $\mathsf{S i O}_{2},$ as shown in Table 1. The characteristic adsorption–regeneration profiles for the PBR filled with inert $\mathsf{S i C}_{4}$ are represented by the red line, which determines the dead volume of the PBR. However, the regeneration profile shows an interesting difference in terms of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ desorption for the composite incorporating MOF nanocrystals. Larger concentrations of early released adsorbate are measured for the $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ hybrid sorbent, which is attributed to weakly adsorbed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ via physisorption because the temperature required to release them is lower than $80~^{\circ}C.$ .
PEI $\mathsf{\Pi}^{\prime}\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ also exhibited an early release, but it is mainly attributed to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ trapped in the dead volume of the PBR, as suggested by comparison with the profile for the inert $\mathsf{S i C}_{4}$ . In addition, $\mathsf{P E l/M O F/S i O}_{2}$ exhibited higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ desorption between 80 and $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which suggests slightly better use of the PEI amines for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ chemisorption as well. This result highlights the unusual dual adsorption performance of our hybrid sorbents containing MOF nanocrystals compared with the pure silica counterpart.
