Fluidized hybrid sorbents containing zinc imidazolates, such as (Zn)ZIF-8 and (Zn)ZIF-7, have demonstrated good ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity under simulated flue gas conditions because they exhibited $140\%$ higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity than the reference PEI impregnated on bare mesoporous silica (Table 1, entry 1) and the lowest 10-cycle deactivation (Table 1, entries 12 and 17) compared with other MOFs. This finding encourages us to measure these two sorbents for long-term stability in a PBR. Both sorbents exhibited excellent stability for 250 cycles under simulated flue gas conditions, as shown in Fig. 6 and resumed in Table S3 in the Supplemental Information.
Flue gas from coal-fired power plants typically contains other acid-gas impurities, such as $S O_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N O}_{\mathsf{x}},$ that can dramatically influence the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture efficiency23, 24. Therefore, during this current work, the performance of our MOF–based sorbents in the presence of those contaminants were evaluated. The results show a clear deactivation of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the sorbents under the presence of $S O_{2}$ . This deactivation is because of the irreversible reaction occurring during the adsorption step between $\mathsf{S O}_{2}$ and PEI amines, which are not further active for the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture (Fig. 6).
Amine deactivation was proportional to the $S O_{2}$ concentration, as shown by the S content determined by CHMS elementary analysis after 100 cycles (see Table S4 in the Supplemental Information). Also, FTIR spectra of these samples measured after 100 cycle testing revealed clear, irreversible binding of $\mathsf{S O}_{2}$ to the hybrid sorbent (Fig. S7 in the Supplemental Information). However, excellent stability at higher $\mathsf{N O}_{\mathsf{x}}$ concentrations was observed. Therefore, MOF nanocrystals within the hybrid solid sorbent did not reduce the tendency of PEI amines to be deactivated by irreversible binding with $S O_{2},$ as similar deactivation for $\mathsf{P E I}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ was measured by our group.18 Therefore, another unit should be added upstream for scrubbing the $S O_{2}$ levels in the flue gas down to a single-digit parts per million level before reaching the FBR to elongate the life of the hybrid solid sorbents and reduce the make-up rate. Good stability was measured in the presence of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ , typically found in sour gas streams, although an initial drop in capacity was observed because the study was performed under dry conditions and without $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ because ${\sf H}_{2}$ was in the $H_{2}S$ cylinder.
## FBR
The fluidizability of the PEI-impregnated $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ solid sorbents and their performance under realistic conditions are of high importance to minimize unexpected operational issues to further scaling-up this technology. In this work, we report the first example of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture from simulated flue gas in a fluidized bed configuration by using a MOF–based ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ solid sorbent. Application of these materials in this confViigeuwrAartiicloenOnhlianes never been proposed because of poor attrDitOiIo:n10a.1n0d39h/aCn7SdCli0n5g3,72aJs well as a lack of fluidizability of bulk MOFs. In doing so, we have developed an experimental setup consisting of a glass-type column. The column works as an absorber and as an regenerator, where the fluidization of the sorbent is carried out with the gas stream25 (see the Supporting Information), which permits the visual evaluation of the sorbent fluidizability.
During this study, the $\mathsf{P E l}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ sorbent and the most promising PEI/MOF/ $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ sorbent (i.e. $4.6\mathrm{wt}\%$ of $[Z\mathsf{n}]Z!\mathsf{F}{\cdot}8/\mathsf{S i O}_{2})$ were exposed to a series of testing conditions to evaluate their fluidizability. First, the sorbents were subjected to an adsorption–regeneration cycle to determine their ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption breakthrough curves. Fig. 7b shows the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ breakthrough curve for both sorbents during the adsorption step compared with bare $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ , which allows the dead volume of the system to be determined.
The breakthrough for the bare $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ occurred after approximately $30~\mathsf{s},$ whereas the use of $_{50\ {\tt g}}$ of solid sorbent containing PEI resulted in a significantly larger lag-time in breakthrough (ca. 6 to $7\min$ ), suggesting that both sorbents are capable of deep ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ scrubbing, close to $100\%$ efficiency, before saturation. As observed in the results obtained in the PBR (Table
1), the sorbent containing MOF nanocrystals also exhibited higher adsorption capacity than PEI $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ in the vFBR.
Afterwards, both sorbents were exposed to a stream containing $80\ \mathrm{v/v\%}$ of $H_{2}O$ balanced with ${\sf N}_{2}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . These very aggressive conditions in the regeneration step were used because the stability under these conditions is crucial for further application, as the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the sorbent could significantly drop because of the amine leaching under steam stripping. It is important to note that the extremely long exposure time of $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ used for this experiment does not represent the actual residence times of the fluidized sorbent in the regenerator, which is typically 2 to 5 min. Fig. 7c is a photograph of the sorbent bed during the initial ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ loading test and after exposure to steam stripping for $^{1\mathfrak{h}}$ . Surprisingly, PEI $S_{\mathrm{i}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ showed a more severe amine leaching in comparison with the material containing MOF nanocrystals under these conditions. Moreover, PEI $S_{\mathrm{i}}{\mathsf O}_{2}$ sorbent became yellow and wet and stuck to the internal reactor wall, completely losing its fluidizability. In contrast, the MOF–containing sorbent appeared to be more resilient to the steam stripping because the material maintained its fluidizability over $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ of testing. These results are consistent with the breakthrough adsorption curves measured for these two sorbents after the steam exposure.
After a drying treatment with 1 SLPM of ${\sf N}_{2}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2\mathsf{h},$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity was practically maintained for PEI/MOF $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{S i O}_{2},}$ whereas a significant drop was observed for the MOF–free sorbent (see Fig. 7b). This improved steam stability of the sorbents containing ZIF-8 nanocrystals can be attributed to the well-known hydrophobicity of the MOFs, which can be enhanced further by using longer aliphatic substituents of the imidazole ring.26 Currently, the influence on the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture performance of other substituents at the imidazole ring of PEI/MOF/ $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ sorbents is under investigation by our group.
Finally, the pressure drops across the FBR reactor bed for all the materials was also examined by varying the ${\sf N}_{2}$ flow from 0.5 to $4.8\ L/\min$ (SLPM). The sorbent containing MOF nanocrystals exhibited a slightly higher pressure drop than PEI/ $\mathsf{\mathsf{S i O}}_{2},$ , which can be attributed to the higher density because of the presence of the MOF $(0.75~\mathrm{kg}/\mathrm{m}^{3}$ for PEI/MOF/ $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ versus $0.68~\mathsf{k g}/\mathsf{m}^{3}$ for $\mathsf{P E I}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2})$ . The main benefits of using denser fluidized sorbents are the reduction of the column size, the increment of the gas velocity and the lower sorbent carried over by flue gas. Nevertheless, an excessive pressure drop in the system can lead to a higher energy requirement to drive the fluidized sorbent through the bed. Fortunately, the sorbents containing moderate MOF loadings demonstrate an equilibrated combination of opposite features in terms of fluidizability when operating at the optimal gas velocity range $(0.1-0.2\ \mathsf{m}/\mathsf{s}^{27})$ (i.e. denser particles, but not involving drastic pressure drops).
## Scale-up and cost analysis study
During this work, we also demonstrated the scale-up of the approach for preparing our novel fluidized hybrid sorbents because up to $1~\mathsf{k g}$ of material was prepared by following our recent published solid-state synthesis approach11 (photographs of the big batch are in Fig. S10 in the SViuepwpAlrteiclmeeOnltinael Information). In addition, the use of inDeOxI:p1e0n.1s0iv39e/Cc7hSeC0m5i3c7a2lJs makes the synthesis very cost effective compared with costly bulk MOF consisting of expensive ligands and polyamines, such as mmen used for $\ensuremath{\mathsf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}_{2}$ (dobpdc).
The price determined in our group for the kilogram-scale preparation of these sorbents is approximately $\$20/123$ according to the cost evaluation study we recently conducted (Fig. S11 in the Supplemental Information). This cost can be reduced to $\$15/123$ by implementing some cost-saving measures such as recycling solvents and finding alternative mesoporous silica and PEI vendors. That reduced cost is in the same range as the production cost for some limited bulk MOFs via liquid assisted grinding (LAG), as recently reported in literature.28
Future directions toward more stable fluidized solid sorbents: Polyamine covalently bonded to MOF/ $\mathsf{s i o}_{2}$ hybrid materials
Using pure steam for the regeneration stage is desired for intensifying the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture process from post-combustion flue gas because $H_{2}O$ can be easily condensed, leading to a pure ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ stream. Nevertheless, steam can progressively displace or wash out active polyamines from current solid sorbents based on PEIimpregnated fluidized sorbents. This limitation may also affect the long-term stability of our novel hybrid solid sorbents during the regeneration stage in an FBR using pure steam because of the relatively weak interactions between MOF nanocrystals and polyamines because $H_{2}O$ molecules can displace coordinated amines as previously discussed for mmen-Mg2(dobpdc). Therefore, an efficient approach for covalent bonding of polyamines to fluidized solids is a high priority.
During the past decade, a wide collection of strategies for post-synthesis modification have been developed for MOFs containing free functional groups at the organic ligand, especially for amino functionality at the aminotherephthalate ligand.24 However, to the best of our knowledge, an efficient, one-pot, environmentally friendly and general approach for covalently bridging amine to amine is still missed in the MOF chemistry. However, the use of THP as a coupling reagent for the immobilization of enzymes onto chitosan films via Mannichtype condensation resulted in greater enzyme performance compared with more conventional coupling routes involving glutaraldehyde.23, 29 Hence, we applied this efficient approach of creating strong covalent bonds amine-to-amine via a methylene phosphine bridge or bridges (N-P-N) to link PEI to $(\mathsf{Z r})\mathsf{U i O}\mathsf{-}66(\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2})$ nanocrystals while maintaining all of the resulting amines still active for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption. This approach is in contrast to conventional glutaraldehyde that form weak imine bonds, which do not exhibit ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ chemisorption (see Fig. 8).
An optimal phosphine grafting $99\%$ by XRF) was achieved via gas-phase phosphine functionalization and the material maintained mostly of the PEI impregnated $95\%$ by elementary analysis) after thoroughly washing the material with methanol after 10 cycles, as shown in Fig. 8. This approach provides a powerful synthetic tool to bind amine-containing organic or organometallic compounds to amine-containing MOFs or other solid supports, preserving their activity to be subsequently used for a variety of applications. The applications range from gas adsorption or separation to heterogeneous catalysis to bioimaging or drug delivery.
## Conclusions
MOFs have been successfully engineered into a fluidized form via solid-state synthesis within mesoporous silica, demonstrating excellent fluidizability and handling and improved attrition resistance. $M O F/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ hybrid materials containing a moderate loading of Zn imidazolate MOFs (ca. 5 wt%) have been found to be optimal to boost the performance of the $35\ \mathsf{w t\%}$ –impregnated polyamines for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture under simulated flue gas conditions because ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capacity and stability are progressively inhibited by increasing the concentration of MOF nanocrystals.
Long-term stability tests under the presence of contaminants and experiments on a visual FBR showed their excellent stability, fluidizability, and outstanding performance under steam regeneration because of the hydrophobicity conferred by the presence of MOF nanocrystals.
